  "summary": "Synthesized both previous contributions into a refined hybrid approach. The second critique correctly identifies that an LLM call at done-time is the weakest link — but its proposed alternative (a lessons_learned field written during implementation) has its own blind spot: implementation agents are optimizing for code correctness, not retrospection, and won't reliably produce useful lessons mid-task. The better design is a three-layer approach: (1) Capture structured signals mechanically at done-time with zero LLM involvement — extract the plan summary, review feedback snippets, and any impl_notes directly from the feature JSON and append them as a raw structured block to BOARD_CONTEXT.md. This is deterministic, cheap, and never hallucinates. (2) Use the existing done_script mechanism as the single integration point rather than hooking three transition sites — create a built-in 'board-context-append' script type that runs alongside user-configured done scripts, which solves the fragility concern since any new done-transition path already calls execute_done_script(). (3) For size management, skip auto-summarization entirely — instead, when BOARD_CONTEXT.md exceeds a threshold (e.g., 30KB, well under the existing 100KB MAX_CONTEXT_SIZE), surface a warning in the TUI and add a 'Curate board context' action that opens the file in the user's editor. This keeps the human in the loop for quality control while the mechanical extraction handles the grunt work. The key insight is separating the two concerns: capturing raw signal (which should be automated and deterministic) vs. distilling wisdom (which should be human-driven or at minimum human-reviewed). This avoids both the cost/hallucination risk of LLM extraction and the unreliability of asking implementation agents to self-reflect mid-task."
